    "summary": "Epic Eyes of the World jam; Bird Song shines; strong second set",
    "blurb": "The second set delivers seamless segues from Truckin' through The Other One Jam into a soaring Eyes Of The World, with Jerry’s jazzy leads and Keith’s organ texture standing out. Bird Song and the debut of Let It Grow provide memorable moments despite a few first‑set miscues. Overall the show is a high‑energy 1973 performance worth revisiting.",
    "review": "On September 7, 1973 at Nassau Coliseum the Dead rolled out a quintessential early‑’70s set that builds from a solid but uneven first half into one of the era’s most exhilarating second halves. Highlights include a fiery Jack Straw in the opening set and an especially vivid Bird Song that many reviewers call “stellar.” The real magic erupts after the intermission: Here Comes Sunshine launches a marathon that strings together Truckin' → Drums → The Other One Jam → Eyes Of The World, culminating in one of Jerry Garcia’s most praised jazzy solos of the year. The debut of Let It Grow—though a bit sloppy at first—adds historic weight, while Keith Godchaux’s organ and Rhodes tones underpin the improvisations with warmth. Despite some editing quirks on the soundboard source and occasional static on the audience tape, the performance’s energy and musicianship make this show a must‑listen for any Deadhead.",
